% ch-05-ex-06-type-error-failing.glp
%
% ⚠ THIS FILE IS MEANT TO FAIL TO LOAD ⚠
%
% This file presents the §5.7.1 type-error illustration from book p 51,
% byte-exact from the PDF. Loading it should PRODUCE A TYPE-ERROR — that
% rejection is the point. The atoms `a, b, c` violate the declared `NumList`
% element type (`Number`), and the type-checker says so.
%
% Programs included (byte-exact):
%   §5.1.3 (book p 47): NumList ::= [] ; [Number | NumList].   — duplicated inline so the file is self-contained
%   §5.7.1 (book p 51): procedure foo(NumList).
%   §5.7.1 (book p 51): foo([a, b, c]).                        — the trigger
%
% See ch-05-ex-06-type-error-corrected.glp for a re-typed form that loads.

%% NumList type, duplicated inline so the failure surfaces as a type-mismatch
%% on the values rather than as an undefined-type error.
NumList ::= [] ; [Number | NumList].

%% Declare foo as accepting a NumList (typed list of Number).
procedure foo(NumList).

%% Fact `foo([a, b, c]).` — the atoms a, b, c are not Numbers, so this clause
%% violates the declared type. The type-checker reports three Inconsistent
%% path errors when the file is loaded.
foo([a, b, c]).
